{
  "topic": "The science of magnetism and superpowers",
  "language": "en",
  "format": "long",
  "category": "ciencia",
  "title": "Could You CONTROL METAL Like a Superhero? 🧲",
  "hook": "In movies, some superheroes lift cars, bridges and metal ships with a wave of their hand.",
  "presenter": "perro",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "role": "hook",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_hook",
      "narration": "In movies, some superheroes lift cars, bridges and metal ships with a wave of their hand.",
      "on_screen_text": "Moving metal by hand",
      "highlight_words": [
        "metal"
      ],
      "sfx": "impact",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_crane",
      "narration": "Could it happen in real life? The answer may surprise you, because part of it already exists.",
      "on_screen_text": "Is it really possible?",
      "highlight_words": [
        "possible"
      ],
      "sfx": "riser",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_magnet",
      "narration": "I'm Leo, and today we'll uncover the science of magnetism, the invisible force that moves metal.",
      "on_screen_text": "The invisible force",
      "highlight_words": [
        "invisible"
      ],
      "sfx": "pop",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_magnet",
      "narration": "The word magnet comes from Magnesia, a region of ancient Greece where stones that attracted iron were found.",
      "on_screen_text": "Stones from Magnesia",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Magnesia"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_poles",
      "narration": "Every magnet has two poles. Opposite poles attract and like poles push apart, even without touching.",
      "on_screen_text": "Poles that attract",
      "highlight_words": [
        "attract"
      ],
      "sfx": "ding",
      "transition": "cut",
      "chapter": "What is a magnet?"
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_poles",
      "narration": "And if you cut a magnet in half, you don't separate the poles: you get two magnets, each with a north and a south.",
      "on_screen_text": "Always two poles",
      "highlight_words": [
        "poles"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_domains",
      "narration": "Inside iron there are tiny zones that act like little magnets. Normally they point every which way.",
      "on_screen_text": "Tiny magnets",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Tiny"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_domains",
      "narration": "When they all line up the same way, the iron becomes a magnet. That's why a magnet grabs nails but not wood or plastic.",
      "on_screen_text": "All lined up",
      "highlight_words": [
        "lined"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_crane",
      "narration": "Now, the most powerful trick: electricity. A current flowing through a coiled wire creates a magnetic field.",
      "on_screen_text": "Electricity = magnetism",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Electricity"
      ],
      "sfx": "ding",
      "transition": "cut",
      "chapter": "Switch-on magnets"
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_crane",
      "narration": "That's an electromagnet. In scrapyards, cranes with electromagnets lift whole cars and drop them when the current switches off.",
      "on_screen_text": "Cranes that lift cars",
      "highlight_words": [
        "cars"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s11",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_crane",
      "narration": "So lifting a car with magnetism is real. It just takes a lot of energy, not a wave of the hand.",
      "on_screen_text": "Real, but energy-hungry",
      "highlight_words": [
        "energy"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s12",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_train",
      "narration": "Magnets can make trains float, too. The Shanghai maglev train reached about four hundred and thirty kilometers per hour.",
      "on_screen_text": "Floating trains",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Floating"
      ],
      "sfx": "whoosh",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s13",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_train",
      "narration": "Because they don't touch the rails, there's almost no friction. They float a few centimeters above the track.",
      "on_screen_text": "Not touching the rails",
      "highlight_words": [
        "rails"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s14",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_mri",
      "narration": "Hospitals have giant magnets: MRI machines, which use fields of one point five to three teslas.",
      "on_screen_text": "Hospital magnets",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Hospital"
      ],
      "sfx": "ding",
      "transition": "cut",
      "chapter": "The strongest magnets"
    },
    {
      "id": "s15",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_mri",
      "narration": "A fridge magnet is about five thousandths of a tesla. An MRI is hundreds of times stronger.",
      "on_screen_text": "Hundreds of times stronger",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Hundreds"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s16",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_mri",
      "narration": "Thanks to those giant magnets, doctors can see inside the body without making a single cut.",
      "on_screen_text": "Seeing inside",
      "highlight_words": [
        "inside"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s17",
      "role": "surprise",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_frog",
      "narration": "And with an even stronger magnet, about sixteen teslas, scientists made a live frog float.",
      "on_screen_text": "A floating frog!",
      "highlight_words": [
        "frog"
      ],
      "sfx": "riser",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s18",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_frog",
      "narration": "It was unharmed. The water in its body is very weakly pushed away by magnets, and with a field that strong, it's enough to float.",
      "on_screen_text": "Water gets pushed away",
      "highlight_words": [
        "pushed"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s19",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_blood",
      "narration": "Could a magnet control a person through the iron in their blood? No, that iron is spread out in molecules and barely responds to magnets.",
      "on_screen_text": "Blood doesn't stick",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Blood"
      ],
      "sfx": "ding",
      "transition": "cut",
      "chapter": "What about the body?"
    },
    {
      "id": "s20",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_blood",
      "narration": "That's why you can go into an MRI without your blood flying out. Loose metal, though, stays outside the room.",
      "on_screen_text": "Safe inside the MRI",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Safe"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s21",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_core",
      "narration": "And the biggest magnet you know is under your feet. Earth's core has moving liquid iron that generates a magnetic field.",
      "on_screen_text": "Earth is a magnet",
      "highlight_words": [
        "magnet"
      ],
      "sfx": "ding",
      "transition": "cut",
      "chapter": "The biggest magnet"
    },
    {
      "id": "s22",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_north",
      "narration": "That's why compasses point north. And that field shields us from particles coming from the Sun.",
      "on_screen_text": "Compasses point north",
      "highlight_words": [
        "north"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s23",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_moving",
      "narration": "Also, the magnetic north pole isn't fixed: it moves tens of kilometers every year.",
      "on_screen_text": "The pole moves",
      "highlight_words": [
        "moves"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s24",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "aur_shield",
      "narration": "When those particles hit the air near the poles, the sky lights up with auroras.",
      "on_screen_text": "Auroras are born",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Auroras"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s25",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_animals",
      "narration": "Some animals do have a magnetic superpower: birds and sea turtles sense Earth's field to find their way.",
      "on_screen_text": "Animals with a compass",
      "highlight_words": [
        "compass"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s26",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_flip",
      "narration": "And Earth's field changes: the magnetic poles have flipped many times in the planet's history.",
      "on_screen_text": "The poles flip",
      "highlight_words": [
        "flip"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s27",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_crane",
      "narration": "So moving metal isn't magic: it's electricity and magnetism, the same force that runs motors, trains and hospitals.",
      "on_screen_text": "Not magic, physics",
      "highlight_words": [
        "physics"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s28",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "mag_crane",
      "narration": "Electromagnets are also in doorbells, speakers and the electric motors that run fans and cars.",
      "on_screen_text": "Electromagnets everywhere",
      "highlight_words": [
        "everywhere"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s29",
      "role": "closing",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "ima_outro",
      "narration": "Which superpower should we test with science next? Tell me in the comments.",
      "on_screen_text": "Which superpower next?",
      "highlight_words": [
        "superpower"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    }
  ],
  "description": "We love superheroes who move metal with their minds. What does physics say? Magnets, electromagnets, floating trains, a levitating frog and the giant magnet that is Earth. Original 3D animation; sizes and distances are not to scale.",
